---
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';
import LegalNav from '../../components/LegalNav.astro';

// Written against the code rather than from a template. Every claim here is checkable: the
// permissions are in apps/extension/wxt.config.ts, the two hosts are the only ones in
// host_permissions, and the storage calls in packages/core are storage.local and storage.session —
// never storage.sync, which is the one that would put your settings on a server.

const updated = 'August 19, 2026';
---
<Shell
	title='Privacy — ArenaSwap'
	description='ArenaSwap has no account, no analytics and no server. Your settings stay in your browser, and the only site it talks to is ESPN.'
	path='legal/privacy/'
>
	<div class='container'>
		<div class='page-head'>
			<h1 class='page-title'>Privacy.</h1>
			<p class='page-lede'>
				ArenaSwap has no account, no analytics and no ads. It has no server either, so there is
				nowhere for what you watch to go.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class='reading-shell'>
			<LegalNav current='legal/privacy/' />

			<div class='prose'>
				<p class='legal-updated'>Last updated {updated}</p>

				<h2>What the extension stores</h2>
				<p>
					Your settings and your tab assignments, on the computer you are using. That covers which
					leagues you follow, your favorite teams, sensitivity, cooldown and switch delay, your
					Standby Stream tab, any manual game boosts, and which tab you assigned to which game.
				</p>
				<p>
					All of it stays on that computer. ArenaSwap never syncs any of it, so it does not reach
					your browser account or the other devices you sign into. Uninstalling the extension
					deletes it.
				</p>

				<h2>What it sends, and where</h2>
				<p>
					Two hosts, both ESPN's, and they are the only two the extension is permitted to reach:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><code>site.api.espn.com</code> for live scores, schedules, clocks, odds and win probabilities.</li>
					<li><code>a.espncdn.com</code> for team crests and league marks.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					These are ordinary web requests, so ESPN sees your IP address and which league
					scoreboards ArenaSwap asked for, the same as if you had opened espn.com yourself.
					ArenaSwap attaches no identifier and no account to them. Turn a league off in Settings
					and it stops asking for that league at all.
				</p>
				<p>
					There is no ArenaSwap server. No crash reporter, no telemetry, no usage pings, no
					A/B testing, and no third-party SDK of any kind.
				</p>

				<h2>The permissions it asks for</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Tabs.</strong> To list your open tabs so you can assign games to them, and to
						switch, mute and unmute them. That grants the list of tabs and nothing more. None of
						ArenaSwap's code runs inside the pages you visit.
					</li>
					<li><strong>Storage.</strong> To keep the settings above.</li>
					<li><strong>Notifications.</strong> To tell you a switch happened. Your browser composes and shows them, and nothing leaves the machine.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					On Firefox, ArenaSwap is listed as collecting no data at all, which is the strictest
					option Mozilla offers.
				</p>

				<h2>This website</h2>
				<p>
					No cookies, no analytics, no tracking pixel, and no consent banner, because there is
					nothing to consent to. The site is hosted on GitHub Pages, and GitHub logs requests to
					its servers as part of hosting that. The fonts come from this site rather than from
					somebody else's server.
				</p>
				<p>
					Two things here do reach ESPN from your browser. The demo screens load team crests from
					<code>a.espncdn.com</code>, and
					the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}powerscore/`}>PowerScore page</a> scores real
					games, so it asks for live scoreboards while you have it open.
				</p>

				<h2>Children</h2>
				<p>
					ArenaSwap collects no personal information from anyone, of any age, which includes
					children under 13.
				</p>

				<h2>Changes</h2>
				<p>
					If this statement ever needs to change, the change ships with a version and that
					version's <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}releases/`}>release notes</a> say so. The
					date at the top is the last time anyone edited it.
				</p>

				<h2>Questions</h2>
				<p>
					Open an issue on <a href='https://github.com/hiteacheryouare/arenaswap/issues'>GitHub</a>.
					The source is public, so you are also welcome to check any of the above for yourself.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</Shell>
